        <div id="three-vs-four" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. 3-Wire vs 4-Wire Dryer Connections, Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            An electric dryer runs on two kinds of power at once. The heating element uses 240 volts across the two hot legs, while the motor, timer, controls and drum light use 120 volts between one hot leg and neutral. That is why a dryer circuit has at least three current-carrying wires, and why the plug cannot be an ordinary household plug. The question that separates a 3-prong from a 4-prong outlet is what happens to the fourth job: keeping the metal cabinet at ground potential if something inside faults.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 3-prong NEMA 10-30 outlet has two hots and a neutral, and nothing else. The dryer's metal cabinet is tied to the neutral through a small metal strap or a green screw on the terminal block. Under normal conditions the neutral sits close to ground, so the cabinet is safe to touch. The weakness shows up when the neutral breaks or loosens somewhere between the dryer and the panel. The neutral current then has nowhere to go, and the cabinet can rise toward 120 volts. You find out by touching the dryer and the washer at the same time.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 4-prong NEMA 14-30 outlet adds a separate equipment ground. Neutral carries the 120-volt return current, and the ground wire does nothing unless there is a fault, at which point it gives fault current a low-resistance path back to the panel and trips the breaker. The bonding strap in the dryer comes out, so the neutral and the cabinet are no longer connected. A broken neutral now stops the dryer from working rather than electrifying it.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can tell the outlets apart at a glance. A 10-30R has three slots, two angled and one L-shaped, and no round hole. A 14-30R has two straight vertical slots, an L-shaped neutral slot, and a round or D-shaped ground hole. The dryer plugs follow suit. If you have a gas dryer, none of this applies: gas dryers use an ordinary 120-volt 3-prong household plug, and you should never put a 30-amp adapter anywhere near one.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers That Matter</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">30A</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Rating of every dryer cord, outlet and adapter</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">10 AWG</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Minimum copper conductor size</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1996</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">NEC edition that required 4-wire for new circuits</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$20-$35</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical price of a replacement cord</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The practical upshot is simple. If your wall has a 14-30R, your dryer should have a 4-wire cord with the bonding strap removed. If your wall has a 10-30R and the circuit is allowed to stay that way, your dryer should have a 3-wire cord with the bonding strap installed. Every situation in the table below comes back to that rule, and an adapter is only ever a way of postponing it.
          </p>

