        <div id="nec-code" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What the NEC Actually Requires
          </h2>

          <p>
            The National Electrical Code, in section 250.140, has required a 4-wire circuit with a separate equipment ground for new dryer and range circuits since the 1996 edition. The 2023 NEC, which most states have adopted or are adopting, keeps that rule. Any new dryer circuit, any circuit extended to a new location, and any new home must be 4-wire, which is why nearly every house built from the late 1990s onward has a 14-30R in the laundry room.
          </p>

          <p>
            What the code does not do is force existing 3-wire circuits to be ripped out. Section 250.140 keeps an exception for existing branch circuits, which allows the neutral to continue serving as the grounding path when certain conditions are met: the neutral must be an insulated conductor of adequate size, at least 10 AWG copper or 8 AWG aluminum, and a bare neutral is only acceptable when the circuit starts at the service equipment rather than at a subpanel. A 1970s house with a properly installed 10-30R that still meets those conditions is generally allowed to keep it.
          </p>

          <p>
            That exception is why the 3-wire cord swap is legitimate. The code permits the existing circuit, the dryer manufacturer permits a 3-wire connection to it, and every installation manual explains how to install the bonding strap for it. An adapter is in a grayer spot. It is not part of the dryer's listed installation, it adds a connection the manual never anticipated, and inspectors and insurers tend to view a semi-permanent adapter behind an appliance unfavorably. Check your dryer's manual: most say plainly that adapters and extension cords should not be used.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two newer rules are worth knowing. The 2020 NEC extended GFCI protection to 250-volt receptacles in dwelling laundry areas, and the 2023 edition carries that forward, so new or replaced dryer outlets often need a 2-pole GFCI breaker where those editions are adopted. The 2023 edition also clarified that an existing 3-wire dryer circuit may remain when service equipment is relocated, a common situation in panel upgrades. Local amendments vary, so your city or county building department is the final word.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Rule of Thumb</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Match the cord to the outlet, not the outlet to the cord.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The outlet tells you what the wiring in the wall can do. The dryer can be configured for either. Changing the dryer side is cheap, reversible and manufacturer-approved. Changing the wall side is only meaningful when the wiring behind it supports a 4-wire connection.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="adapters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Truth About 3 Prong to 4 Prong Dryer Adapters
          </h2>

          <p>
            Search for a dryer converter and you will find hundreds of adapters and pigtails, most from brands that did not exist five years ago. Many are made with genuine 10 AWG cord and molded plugs, and some carry real UL or ETL listing marks. Many others claim the cord is rated to a UL standard, which is not the same as the finished adapter being listed. Look for the mark and file number printed on the cord jacket or molded into the plug, not just in the product title.
          </p>

          <p>
            Even a perfectly made adapter has two built-in limits. First, the 3-prong-outlet direction cannot add a ground. A 10-30R has no ground conductor behind it, so the 14-30R side of the adapter either ties its ground pin to neutral internally or gives you a loose green wire to attach somewhere. Electrically, the best case is identical to a 3-wire cord with the bonding strap installed, and the worst case is a ground pin connected to nothing, with your dryer's bonding strap removed because the dryer thinks it is on a 4-wire circuit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Second, every adapter adds a plug-and-socket joint carrying up to 30 amps. Those joints are where heat builds when contact tension weakens, when the plug is not fully seated, or when the dryer is pushed back and levers the adapter sideways. A dryer runs its heater for an hour at a time, often unattended, and the adapter is wedged behind it where nobody sees it discolor. That is a poor place for an avoidable connection.
          </p>

          <p>
            So when is an adapter reasonable? When the arrangement is genuinely short-term, when you are not permitted to open the dryer or the wall, or as a bridge for a few days until the right cord arrives. If you use one, choose a short lead rated 30 amps and 250 volts with 10 AWG conductors and a visible listing mark, seat it fully, keep it clear of the dryer feet, and check it for warmth or discoloration after the first few loads. Then plan the cord swap.
          </p>

          <p>
            One adapter category deserves a flat no: anything that converts a dryer outlet to feed something else, or a range, welder or generator outlet to feed a dryer, without a matched amp rating. Plugging a 30-amp dryer into a 50-amp range outlet through an adapter means the breaker will not trip until well past what the dryer cord can safely carry. If the generator is the reason you are shopping for adapters, read our <a href="/generator-extension-cord-size/">generator extension cord sizing guide</a> and our explainer on <a href="/interlock-kit-vs-transfer-switch/">interlock kits vs transfer switches</a> instead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="swap-cord" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Swap a Dryer Cord Safely
          </h2>

          <p>
            Swapping a dryer cord is one of the most approachable electrical jobs in a house, because you never touch the wiring in the wall. You need a 5/16-inch or 1/4-inch nut driver or a Phillips screwdriver, the new cord, a strain relief clamp if one is not included, and about 20 minutes. Unplug the dryer before you start. If you cannot reach the plug, switch off the 2-pole 30-amp breaker, and use our guide on <a href="/how-to-find-which-breaker-controls-an-outlet/">finding which breaker controls an outlet</a> if the panel is not labeled.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step by step</h3>

          <p>
            Pull the dryer out far enough to work behind it and remove the small cover plate over the terminal block, usually one screw near where the cord enters. Take a photo before you disconnect anything. Loosen the strain relief, remove the three terminal nuts and, on a 4-wire cord, the green ground screw, and slide the old cord out. Note the bonding strap or green bonding screw that connects the center neutral terminal to the cabinet.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a 4-wire cord, remove the bonding strap or green bonding screw, or fold it back as your manual describes, and attach the green conductor to the cabinet ground screw with its ring terminal. Connect white to the center post and the two hots to the outer posts. For a 3-wire cord, make sure the bonding strap is installed between the center terminal and the cabinet, then connect the center conductor to the center post and the outer conductors to the outer posts. On most 3-wire cords the conductors are not color-coded, and the middle one in the flat cord is neutral.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tighten each terminal nut firmly so the ring is clamped flat and cannot rotate, but do not crank hard enough to crack the terminal block. Fit the strain relief so the cord jacket, not the conductors, is gripped where it passes through the cabinet, reinstall the cover plate, and plug in. Run a short timed-dry cycle and confirm both heat and tumbling. No heat with tumbling usually means one hot leg is loose or not connected.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the old cord in a labeled bag inside the laundry cabinet or taped to the back of the dryer. When you move, the next laundry room may need it. And write the configuration on a piece of masking tape near the terminal block cover so the next person to work on the dryer knows whether the bonding strap should be in or out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="replace-outlet" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. When Replacing the Outlet Is the Better Fix
          </h2>

          <p>
            Changing a 10-30R to a 14-30R is worthwhile in exactly one situation: the cable behind it already contains a proper ground conductor. That happens in homes wired during the transition years, in renovations where an electrician pulled modern cable but reused an old receptacle, and in apartments where the building was upgraded unevenly. Turn off the breaker, pull the receptacle, and count the conductors. Two insulated hots, an insulated neutral and a separate bare or green ground means you are in luck.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you find only three conductors, or a service-entrance style cable whose neutral is a bare braided wrap, there is no ground to connect, and installing a 14-30R would be cosmetic at best. Jumping the ground terminal to the neutral inside the box, sometimes called a bootleg ground, is a code violation and arguably worse than a plainly labeled 3-wire outlet, because it tells the next owner the circuit is safer than it is.
          </p>

          <p>
            Box size is the other surprise. Old 10-30R receptacles often sit in small single-gang boxes, while most 14-30R receptacles need a two-gang box or a surface-mount housing. Surface-mount 14-30R units sidestep the box problem on unfinished walls. Torque matters on 30-amp terminals: follow the receptacle's marked torque value if your screwdriver can measure it, and if you use a separate tool for stripping, make sure it is sized for 10 AWG, like the ones in our <a href="/best-wire-stripper/">best wire strippers</a> roundup.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, a replaced dryer receptacle may trigger the GFCI requirement in jurisdictions on the 2020 or 2023 NEC, which typically means a 2-pole GFCI breaker in the panel. Older dryers occasionally nuisance-trip GFCI breakers because of small leakage currents in heating elements, so ask the electrician or inspector how your local office handles it before you buy parts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What to Check Before You Buy a Cord or Adapter
          </h2>

          <p>
            Amp rating comes first. Standard electric dryers use 30-amp circuits, and every cord, adapter and receptacle in the chain should be rated 30 amps at 125/250 volts. A 50-amp range cord is not an upgrade for a dryer; the oversized plug will not fit a dryer outlet, and if it is forced onto a 50-amp circuit the breaker no longer protects the dryer's internal wiring. Some large or commercial dryers have different requirements, so check the nameplate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wire gauge comes second. A 30-amp dryer cord should have 10 AWG copper conductors, printed on the jacket as 10/3 or 10/4. Listings that say heavy duty without giving the gauge are not telling you what you need to know. Terminal type comes third: closed ring terminals are more forgiving than forked spades because they cannot slide off a loosened stud, which is why most current manuals prefer or require them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Length should be the shortest that reaches comfortably, usually 4 feet and occasionally 6. Never use an extension cord on a dryer, and do not chain an adapter onto an extension cord. Strain relief should be in the box or bought alongside, because a cord without it eventually rubs through on the cabinet knockout. And look for a listing mark from a recognized lab such as UL, ETL or CSA on the product itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are buying a new dryer, add the cord to the same order. Most retailers will sell you the correct cord and many will install it during delivery if you tell them which outlet you have. If you are shopping for the dryer itself, our <a href="/best-heat-pump-dryer/">best heat pump dryer</a> guide covers ventless models, several of which run on ordinary 120-volt outlets and skip the 30-amp question entirely.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="electrician" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. When to Call an Electrician
          </h2>

          <p>
            A cord swap is a homeowner job. Anything inside the wall or the panel is a different category of risk, and on a 30-amp 240-volt circuit the margin for error is thin. Call a licensed electrician if the outlet needs to move, if the circuit needs a ground conductor, if the breaker is not a 2-pole 30-amp breaker, or if the receptacle or cord shows scorching, melting or a burnt smell.
          </p>

          <p>
            Aluminum branch wiring, which shows up in some homes built in the late 1960s and early 1970s, is another reason to stop. Aluminum conductors need receptacles and terminals rated for aluminum and careful torque. A visibly loose or cracked 10-30R, a breaker that trips when the dryer starts, or lights that dim sharply when the heater cycles all point to connection problems that an adapter or a new cord will not fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            Running a new 10/3 with ground circuit from the panel to the laundry area typically costs a few hundred dollars for a short, accessible run, more if walls must be opened or the panel is full. That buys a permanent, inspectable, code-current connection, and it is the only way to get a true equipment ground to a laundry room that never had one. Ask for a permit. It protects you at resale and with your insurer.
          </p>

          <p>
            While the electrician is there, it is a good time to sort out labeling and protection. Our guides to <a href="/how-to-label-a-breaker-panel/">labeling a breaker panel</a> and the <a href="/best-whole-house-surge-protector/">best whole-house surge protectors</a> cover two low-cost add-ons that are easiest to handle during the same visit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="renters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Renters, Movers and Short-Term Setups
          </h2>

          <p>
            Renters are the biggest audience for 4 prong to 3 prong dryer adapters, and they have the most constraints. You usually cannot change the outlet, and you may not be allowed to hire an electrician. But the dryer is your property, and swapping its cord is not a modification to the building. A 3-wire cord with the bonding strap installed is the cleanest way to connect your 4-prong dryer to an apartment's 10-30R, and you keep the 4-wire cord for the next place.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your lease or your landlord's maintenance team has rules about appliances, tell them what you are doing. Most will be happier with a correctly configured cord than with an adapter behind the dryer. If the outlet is damaged, loose in the wall or discolored, report it in writing rather than working around it. That is a landlord repair, and an adapter plugged into a failing receptacle only moves the failure point.
          </p>

          <p>
            Moving with a dryer is when cord mismatches surface, usually on the day the truck arrives. Before you move, check the outlet at the new place, or ask the landlord or seller for a photo, and buy whichever cord you need in advance. A $25 cord delivered before moving day beats a $50 adapter bought at the hardware store at closing time.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the new place has no 240-volt outlet at all, no cord or adapter will help, and you are looking at a different class of machine. Compact 120-volt dryers and ventless heat pump models are the usual answers, and our <a href="/best-heat-pump-dryer/">heat pump dryer guide</a> explains which ones plug into an ordinary wall outlet. Do not try to run a full-size electric dryer from a 120-volt receptacle through any adapter. It will not work, and some of the adapters sold for it are dangerous.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using an adapter as the permanent fix</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An adapter adds a 30-amp plug joint behind a machine nobody looks at, and the 3-prong-outlet direction cannot add a ground. A $25 replacement cord gives the same or better result with fewer connections. Use the adapter for days or weeks, not years, and plan the cord swap from the start.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the bonding strap</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Going from 4-wire to 3-wire means reinstalling the strap or green screw between neutral and cabinet. Going from 3-wire to 4-wire means removing it. Getting this backward is the most common cord-swap error and can leave the cabinet ungrounded or put neutral current on the ground wire. Photograph the block first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Bootlegging a ground at the outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Installing a 14-30R on a three-conductor circuit and jumping the ground terminal to the neutral is a code violation. It looks like a modern installation while behaving like an old one, and it hides the risk from the next owner or inspector. No ground in the box means a new cable, not a jumper.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a dryer on an extension cord</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ordinary extension cords are not rated for 30 amps at 240 volts, and even heavy cords add more joints and more length to overheat. Every dryer manual forbids it. If the cord does not reach, buy a 6-foot cord or have the outlet moved by an electrician rather than bridging the gap.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by price instead of gauge</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cheap adapters and cords sometimes skimp on conductor size or use spade terminals that loosen. Look for 10 AWG copper printed on the jacket, closed ring terminals, a 30-amp rating and a real UL, ETL or CSA mark on the product. A vague listing that says heavy duty without a gauge is a pass.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 4 prong to 3 prong dryer adapter safe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A well-made, listed adapter is safe enough as a short-term bridge if it is rated 30 amps, uses 10 AWG wire, and is fully seated. It is not as safe as the alternative, which is swapping the dryer cord to match the outlet. That costs about $25, removes an extra plug joint, and is the method every dryer manual describes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I put a 4 prong cord on a 3 prong outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not directly. Either swap the dryer to a 3-wire cord with the bonding strap installed, which is the manufacturer-approved method for an existing 3-wire circuit, or use a 10-30P to 14-30R adapter as a stopgap. Neither creates a true ground. Only a 4-wire circuit with a ground conductor back to the panel does that.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I have to upgrade my 3 prong dryer outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. The NEC has required 4-wire circuits for new dryer installations since 1996, but section 250.140 lets qualifying existing 3-wire circuits remain. Extending or relocating the circuit, or a local amendment, can trigger an upgrade. Your building department can tell you which code edition and amendments apply where you live.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a 10-30 and a 14-30 outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 10-30 outlet has three slots: two hots and a neutral, with no ground. A 14-30 outlet has four: two hots, a neutral and a separate equipment ground. Both are rated 30 amps at 125/250 volts. New dryer circuits must use 14-30 receptacles, while 10-30 receptacles survive in many homes wired before the late 1990s.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take to change a dryer cord?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                About 20 minutes for most people, most of it spent pulling the dryer out and removing the terminal cover. You need a nut driver or screwdriver, the new cord and a strain relief clamp. The critical step is setting the bonding strap correctly for a 3-wire or 4-wire connection, so photograph the terminal block before you start.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I change the outlet from 3 prong to 4 prong myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the box already contains a separate ground conductor that runs back to the panel, and only if you are comfortable working on a 240-volt circuit with the breaker off. Many jurisdictions also require a permit and GFCI protection for replaced laundry receptacles. If there is no ground wire in the box, an electrician needs to run new cable.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a gas dryer need a 4 prong adapter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Gas dryers use a standard 120-volt three-prong household plug for the motor, controls and igniter, and plug into an ordinary 15 or 20-amp outlet. They never use a 30-amp dryer outlet. If someone suggests an adapter for a gas dryer, the problem is somewhere else, most often a missing outlet near the gas line.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

